WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.600
What is model identifiability in Machine Learning?

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.400
A model is identifiable, if with&nbsp;
a sufficiently large training set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.040
we can rule out all but one&nbsp;
parameter setting of the model.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:19.040
Non-identifiability is the opposite when&nbsp;
multiple equivalent models give the same outputs.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.640
In a nutshell: There are many causes for&nbsp;
model non-identifiability: for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.520
weight space symmetry, which happens when&nbsp;
we rearrange the hidden units and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.480
incoming and outgoing weight vectors accordingly.&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.960
We get technically a different model,&nbsp;
but one delivering the same output.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.440
Model nonidentifiability could&nbsp;
cause infinitely many equivalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.560
model configurations and therefore&nbsp;
potentially infinitely many local minima.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.720
But this is not THE big problem for non-convex&nbsp;
optimization because at least these local minima&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.480
have equivalent value, so the bad and the&nbsp;
good local minima proliferate all the same.

